## Semantic highlight with `<mark>`

```html
<p>Search results for "frontend":</p>
<p>She is a <mark>frontend</mark> engineer with 7 years of experience.</p>
```

```css
/* Default browser style */
mark { background-color: yellow; color: black; }

/* Customise if needed */
mark {
  background-color: #ffd700;
  border-radius: 2px;
  padding: 0 2px;
}
```

## When to use each element

| Situation | Element |
|---|---|
| Search term match | `<mark>` |
| Relevant passage in quoted text | `<mark>` |
| Visual callout, no semantic meaning | `<span>` + CSS |
| Important / serious text | `<strong>` |
| Stressed emphasis | `<em>` |

## Accessibility note

`<mark>` is not announced by all screen readers by default. If the highlight is meaningful, add a visually-hidden label:

```css
.sr-only {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip: rect(0 0 0 0);
}
```

```html
<mark>
  <span class="sr-only">[highlight start]</span>
  frontend
  <span class="sr-only">[highlight end]</span>
</mark>
```

## DOM tree structure

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head><title>Hello</title></head>
  <body>
    <h1 id="title">Hello World</h1>
    <p class="intro">Welcome.</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

Tree:
```
Document
└── html
    ├── head
    │   └── title → "Hello"
    └── body
        ├── h1 #title → "Hello World"
        └── p .intro → "Welcome."
```

## Querying the DOM

```js
const heading = document.getElementById('title');
const first   = document.querySelector('.intro');
const items   = document.querySelectorAll('li');
```

## Manipulating the DOM

```js
// Change text
heading.textContent = 'Updated Title';

// Change attributes
heading.setAttribute('class', 'active');
heading.classList.add('highlight');

// Create and insert nodes
const p = document.createElement('p');
p.textContent = 'New paragraph';
document.body.appendChild(p);

// Remove
heading.remove();
```

## DOM vs HTML source

The DOM is live — JavaScript mutations and the browser's own error correction make it diverge from the raw HTML source. `View Source` shows the original HTML; DevTools Elements panel shows the current DOM state.

## Virtual DOM (React context)

React's Virtual DOM is a lightweight in-memory copy of the real DOM. React diffs the virtual tree and applies only the minimal required changes to the real DOM — avoiding expensive full repaints.
